    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ca23134b_f7eb5907",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000239
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-06T15:35:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "+kitching\n\nHi Joel, I think we currently don\u0027t support \u003e32 bit event code right? Or have we enabled 64-bit event code? What\u0027s the right way to add new events here?",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "767a16ad_b1fc9cb0",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1126709
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-07T05:47:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve already cherry-picked most required patches to enable 64-bit host events into eve-campfire branch.  There are a few remaining:\n- CL:1016444\n- CL:1049467\n- CL:1049470\n- CL:1049469\n\nThe correct way to introduce a new host event is to directly commit it (or a placeholder) into ToT, so that we don\u0027t create any conflicting events.\n\nHowever, I am confused -- what is the current method to brighten and dim the screen, and why can we not use that method?",
      "parentUuid": "ca23134b_f7eb5907",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c4d94b31_3b8ffaaf",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003079
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-08T05:38:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For chrome os, it use scancode(key F5/F6) to control brightness.\nBut in campfire, the scancode is only for key F5/F6. And campfire OS doesn\u0027t have brightness conntrol scancode.\nWe need use HOST_EVENT to notify OS control brightness in campfire.",
      "parentUuid": "767a16ad_b1fc9cb0",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "061e558b_de1954b7",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1126709
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-10T13:13:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looks like in Chrome OS, brightness is controlled at an OS/window manager level (see here: https://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/ash/system/brightness/brightness_controller_chromeos.cc?type\u003dcs\u0026q\u003dbrightness\u0026g\u003d0\u0026l\u003d19).\n\nHow does this generally work in other OSs?  Is a separate driver needed to control the screen brightness?  Is this driver (or something else) going to be polling EC host events to figure out when brightness should go up and down?",
      "parentUuid": "c4d94b31_3b8ffaaf",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7bcb7df9_518b2c8d",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003079
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-11T02:14:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "BIOS will define host event in ACPI OS.\nWhen EC trigger these events, ACPI OS will check the event and notify driver to do brightness up/down.",
      "parentUuid": "061e558b_de1954b7",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "743c7b1e_e18a3a1b",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1126709
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-11T04:56:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we have this functionality in coreboot/depthcharge ACPI yet?  Or will that be done in a separate CL?  Do other OS\u0027s already have this driver available, or will we also need to write them?",
      "parentUuid": "7bcb7df9_518b2c8d",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0bb248ca_f3ec8fca",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1248957
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-13T03:23:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The relative Q-events have not upload to eve firmware branch yet since we have not receive the Intel new GFX driver for brightness verify,Please refer to issue #72196531. I will upload the coreboot changes tomorrow.",
      "parentUuid": "743c7b1e_e18a3a1b",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9e508802_4a4b5d3e",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1248957
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-15T10:19:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The coreboot change for brightness hotkey control will be uploaded after intel provide how to works with new GFX driver and verify ok. We have not get the new driver yet.",
      "parentUuid": "0bb248ca_f3ec8fca",
      "revId": "4c5820e76025fb8d9d05132a46acdf89efc82bd1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "df062763_989c3e2b",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 582,
      "author": {
        "id": 1284883
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-24T00:37:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Intel has drivers that could handle the brightness (e.g., intel-hid.c).  In ACPI there\u0027s be a device INT33D5 that sends event 0xC0 each time it wants the driver to act.  The driver looks at HDEM as based on the value sends a key event (e.g., 19 for KEY_BRIGHTNESSUP and 20 for KEY_BRIGHTNESSDOWN ). There\u0027s more details in NDA docs from Intel (\"hid event filter driver\").\n\nOn Windows the driver probably creates a virtual HID device and generates the standardized usage events in http://www.usb.org/developers/hidpage/HUTRR41.pdf . That\u0027s a fair amount of work for just toggling screen brightness via keyboard.  We wouldn\u0027t have to write a driver but we\u0027re also limited by what the driver can do (which appears to be constrained to a fixed list of keys [vs supporting a table generated by the firmware]).\n\nI hope the proposal wasn\u0027t to instead just execute the acpi brightness methods directly (and bypass the OS).  I see ways for the OS to be notified for screen mode changes that bypassed the OS, but not for brightness.",
